NAME
       lvextend - make a logical volume bigger

SYNOPSIS
       lvextend {-L [+]SIZE | -l [+]EXTENTS} [-r] LV [PV...]

DESCRIPTION
       lvextend grows a logical volume (LV) using free space in its volume
       group (VG). It is part of LVM, the logical volume manager. The LV can
       stay mounted and in use while it grows.

       Growing the LV does not grow the file system inside it. Either add -r
       (--resizefs), which grows the file system right after the LV, or grow
       the file system yourself: xfs_growfs MOUNTPOINT for XFS, resize2fs
       DEVICE for ext4. Forgetting this step is a common mistake: lvs shows
       the new size but df still shows the old one.

       The LV can be named /dev/VG/LV, /dev/mapper/VG-LV or VG/LV.

       Sizes are rounded up to whole physical extents (4 MiB each unless the
       VG was created with another -s). lvextend says so when it rounds.

       When the VG has no free space left, add a disk or partition to it
       first: pvcreate the device, then vgextend VG DEVICE.

OPTIONS
       -L, --size [+]SIZE
              The new size, with a unit: K, M, G or T (powers of 1024, so 1G
              is 1024 MiB). With a plus sign the size is added to the current
              size: -L +500M grows the LV by 500 MiB, -L 2G makes it 2 GiB in
              total.

       -l, --extents [+]N
              The size in extents instead of bytes. A percentage is allowed:
              -l +100%FREE uses all the free space in the VG, -l +50%FREE
              half of it, -l 80%VG makes the LV 80% of the whole VG.

       -r, --resizefs
              Also grow the file system on the LV (XFS, ext4). XFS must be
              mounted to grow; ext4 can be mounted or not.

       -t, --test
              Show what would happen without changing anything.

       PV...  Take the new extents only from these physical volumes.

EXAMPLES
       Grow the LV data in VG vgdata by 300 MiB and its file system with it:

           lvextend -r -L +300M /dev/vgdata/data

       Make the LV exactly 1 GiB, then grow its XFS file system by hand:

           lvextend -L 1G /dev/vgdata/data
           xfs_growfs /data

       Give an ext4 LV all the remaining free space of its VG:

           lvextend -l +100%FREE vgdata/archive
           resize2fs /dev/vgdata/archive

       Check the result:

           lvs vgdata
           df -h /data

NOTES
       Exam tasks often give a size range ("between 450 and 500 MiB"). Pick
       a size that is a whole number of extents inside the range, such as
       480M with 4 MiB extents or 464M with 16 MiB extents.

       An LV cannot grow past the free space of its VG. Check it first with
       vgs (the VFree column).

